   "evidence_ids": [
    "ev-5fa58511a1e2"
   ]
  }
 ],
 "ruled_out": [
  {
   "statement": "Orders is not the origin even though it reports the most errors. Its 504s match timeouts waiting on inventory, and trace self time is concentrated in inventory.",
   "evidence_ids": [
    "ev-5fa58511a1e2",
    "ev-1da116a62fee"
   ]
  },
  {
   "statement": "The gateway is not the origin. Its 502s mirror orders' 504s on the error path.",
   "evidence_ids": [
    "ev-5fa58511a1e2",
    "ev-c000e6b4f684"
   ]
  },
  {
   "statement": "A traffic surge is unlikely. Request rates stay modest and steady: gateway ~2.1 rps avg (max 2.46), orders and inventory ~1.43 rps avg (max ~1.66).",
   "evidence_ids": [
    "ev-c000e6b4f684",
    "ev-1da116a62fee",
    "ev-51525d7035d0"
   ]
  }
 ],
 "unknowns": [
  "Logs could not be queried (backend timeout), so inventory error or slow-query messages and orders timeout logs are unavailable.",
  "There are no traces from before the deploy to compare POST /reserve latency on 3.1.5 against 3.2.1.",
  "Inventory's downstream dependencies (database, locks) are not covered by this evidence, so the slowness could be in a dependency that 3.2.1 calls differently.",
  "No infrastructure metrics (CPU, memory, connection pools) for inventory are available."
 ],
 "recommended_actions": [
  {
   "action": "Roll back inventory from 3.2.1 to 3.1.5.",
   "rationale": "Onset closely follows the deploy, and trace self time points to inventory POST /reserve on 3.2.1. Rollback is the fastest mitigation and also tests the hypothesis.",
   "evidence_ids": [
    "ev-190058c637be",
    "ev-5fa58511a1e2"
   ]
  },
  {
   "action": "After the rollback, confirm recovery: inventory p95 back under 1s and orders and gateway 5xx ratios below 5%.",
   "rationale": "This validates that the deploy was the cause.",
   "evidence_ids": [
    "ev-51525d7035d0",
